  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nNightly temperatures and resulting frost/freeze conditions take\ncenter stage this week as the area remains nestled beneath high\npressure. Mostly clear skies and weakening low level wind flow\nwill yield efficient radiational cooling conditions to impose a\ncombination of morning frost and river vally fog development\nacross the area.\n\nAfter last night's hard freeze along our mountain counties/zones,\nit was elected that the growing season has come to an end for\nthese locations. Elsewhere in the forecast area, another round\nof frost development is likely to transpire tonight, with\nconfidence of this occurring higher across the north-central\nlowlands and becoming more isolated in nature in the coalfields\nwhere temperatures could stay a few degrees higher than\ncurrently anticipated outside of the sheltered valleys. Opted\nfor a Frost Advisory across all of the remaining growing area\ntonight as temperatures could drop as low as 35 degrees in many\nlocations.\n\nSimilar conditions could be achieved once again Monday night\nbefore overnight temperatures slightly moderate after midweek.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nPredominating upper level ridging and high pressure nestled over\nthe forecast area will advertise a stretch of dry weather that\nis progged to extend into at least next weekend. Daytime\ntemperatures remain steady through the balance of the valid \nforecast period, with afternoon highs reaching the 50s along the\nmountains and mid to upper 60s across the lowlands each day. \nGlobal models hold on to this predominantly dry weather pattern \nwell into the start of next week.",
